WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:01.312
Welcome!

00:00:01.312 --> 00:00:04.686
In this Java tutorial, you'll use inheritance to

00:00:04.686 --> 00:00:07.638
share class behavior, override a method to

00:00:07.638 --> 00:00:10.660
specialize it, and use an abstract class to

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.752
require behavior that each concrete subclass

00:00:13.752 --> 00:00:14.666
must provide.

00:00:15.386 --> 00:00:18.507
Related features in an application often need

00:00:18.507 --> 00:00:21.697
common information but different calculations.

00:00:21.697 --> 00:00:25.025
These tools help you keep the shared work in one

00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:27.938
place while giving each kind of object the

00:00:27.938 --> 00:00:29.186
behavior it needs.

00:00:29.844 --> 00:00:33.077
Today we'll help a campus team plan a reading

00:00:33.077 --> 00:00:33.436
task.

00:00:33.436 --> 00:00:34.873
Each job has a name.

00:00:34.873 --> 00:00:38.322
A reading job also has a nonnegative page count,

00:00:38.322 --> 00:00:41.483
and our planning rule allows two minutes per

00:00:41.483 --> 00:00:41.842
page.

00:00:41.842 --> 00:00:45.219
We'll start with a three-page task named Guide.

00:00:45.881 --> 00:00:49.007
Our program will report a job heading, a reading

00:00:49.007 --> 00:00:51.547
description, and an estimated duration.

00:00:51.547 --> 00:00:54.672
We'll put the shared name in a base class, build

00:00:54.672 --> 00:00:57.538
a reading subclass, and trace how the caller

00:00:57.538 --> 00:00:59.231
reaches the right methods.

00:00:59.918 --> 00:01:02.818
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:01:02.818 --> 00:01:03.493
Main.java.

00:01:07.657 --> 00:01:08.892
The file is open.

00:01:08.892 --> 00:01:12.234
Every job needs a name, but different jobs may

00:01:12.234 --> 00:01:14.196
calculate time differently.

00:01:14.196 --> 00:01:17.392
Let's define an abstract NamedJob class that

00:01:17.392 --> 00:01:20.880
stores the common name without pretending it can

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:23.132
calculate every job's duration.

00:01:33.606 --> 00:01:36.754
Abstract means we cannot directly construct a

00:01:36.754 --> 00:01:37.384
NamedJob.

00:01:37.384 --> 00:01:40.252
The class can still store information and

00:01:40.252 --> 00:01:41.931
provide working methods.

00:01:41.931 --> 00:01:45.078
Its constructor receives a name and copies it

00:01:45.078 --> 00:01:48.017
into the private field of the object being

00:01:48.017 --> 00:01:48.856
initialized.

00:01:48.872 --> 00:01:51.909
A reading subclass will need to use that name,

00:01:51.909 --> 00:01:54.813
but it does not need direct control over the

00:01:54.813 --> 00:01:55.209
field.

00:01:55.209 --> 00:01:57.916
Let's add a protected getter and a public

00:01:57.916 --> 00:02:00.622
description method that returns the name.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.553
GetName returns the stored name without changing

00:02:17.553 --> 00:02:17.756
it.

00:02:17.756 --> 00:02:20.941
Protected makes this operation available in the

00:02:20.941 --> 00:02:23.923
subclass code we'll write; Java also permits

00:02:23.923 --> 00:02:25.888
access from the same package.

00:02:25.888 --> 00:02:27.989
The name field remains private.

00:02:27.989 --> 00:02:31.038
Description provides ordinary public behavior

00:02:31.038 --> 00:02:32.800
that a caller can request.

00:02:32.825 --> 00:02:35.975
We can describe a named job now, but we still

00:02:35.975 --> 00:02:37.165
need an estimate.

00:02:37.165 --> 00:02:40.455
Let's require an integer minutes method without

00:02:40.455 --> 00:02:43.325
choosing a calculation in the base class.

00:02:47.962 --> 00:02:50.416
This declaration has no method body.

00:02:50.416 --> 00:02:53.347
A concrete subclass must supply the missing

00:02:53.347 --> 00:02:56.619
implementation before we can create its objects.

00:02:56.619 --> 00:02:59.754
That lets a caller ask for minutes through the

00:02:59.754 --> 00:03:03.026
shared base type while each kind of job supplies

00:03:03.026 --> 00:03:03.912
its own rule.

00:03:03.931 --> 00:03:07.193
A reading job adds a page count to the shared

00:03:07.193 --> 00:03:07.556
name.

00:03:07.556 --> 00:03:10.891
Let's extend NamedJob, keep pages in a private

00:03:10.891 --> 00:03:13.936
field, and pass the name to the superclass

00:03:13.936 --> 00:03:16.981
constructor before storing the page count.

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:36.534
Extends establishes the superclass relationship.

00:03:36.534 --> 00:03:39.478
In our Java 21 runtime, this explicit super call

00:03:39.478 --> 00:03:41.686
comes first in the constructor body.

00:03:41.686 --> 00:03:44.139
It initializes the base part of the same

00:03:44.139 --> 00:03:46.960
ReadingJob object; it does not create a second

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:47.206
job.

00:03:47.206 --> 00:03:49.904
The next statement stores that object's page

00:03:49.904 --> 00:03:50.272
count.

00:03:50.272 --> 00:03:52.971
Constructors are not inherited, which is why

00:03:52.971 --> 00:03:55.240
ReadingJob supplies this constructor.

00:03:55.269 --> 00:03:57.942
The shared description gives us a name.

00:03:57.942 --> 00:04:01.231
For a reading job, we also want the word reading

00:04:01.231 --> 00:04:01.848
after it.

00:04:01.848 --> 00:04:04.657
Let's override description, call the base

00:04:04.657 --> 00:04:07.810
version explicitly, and add that suffix to the

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:08.769
returned text.

00:04:19.744 --> 00:04:22.813
The Override annotation asks the compiler to

00:04:22.813 --> 00:04:24.906
check the method relationship.

00:04:24.906 --> 00:04:28.254
Inside this body, super.description deliberately

00:04:28.254 --> 00:04:31.253
calls NamedJob's implementation on the same

00:04:31.253 --> 00:04:31.741
object.

00:04:31.741 --> 00:04:34.810
It returns the name, and our override adds a

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:36.624
space followed by reading.

00:04:36.624 --> 00:04:39.344
The stored name itself stays unchanged.

00:04:39.373 --> 00:04:42.491
We have specialized the description, but the

00:04:42.491 --> 00:04:45.821
abstract minutes operation is still unfinished.

00:04:45.821 --> 00:04:47.806
Let's implement it using our

00:04:47.806 --> 00:04:49.648
two-minutes-per-page rule.

00:04:57.378 --> 00:05:00.109
Minutes reads this object's page count and

00:05:00.109 --> 00:05:01.474
multiplies it by two.

00:05:01.474 --> 00:05:04.595
The result represents minutes, because each page

00:05:04.595 --> 00:05:06.155
contributes two minutes.

00:05:06.155 --> 00:05:09.211
For the three-page Guide task, that calculation

00:05:09.211 --> 00:05:09.991
returns six.

00:05:09.991 --> 00:05:13.112
Our accepted inputs are nonnegative page counts;

00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:16.102
this small example relies on that stated limit

00:05:16.102 --> 00:05:18.378
rather than adding validation here.

00:05:18.403 --> 00:05:21.069
We also need a heading for the display.

00:05:21.069 --> 00:05:24.281
Let's add a public heading method that gets the

00:05:24.281 --> 00:05:26.878
inherited name and puts Job before it.

00:05:34.126 --> 00:05:36.919
Heading uses getName from the base class.

00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:39.985
It does not reach into the private name field

00:05:39.985 --> 00:05:40.598
directly.

00:05:40.598 --> 00:05:43.460
The subclass gets the information it needs

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:46.390
through a defined operation, while the base

00:05:46.390 --> 00:05:49.251
remains responsible for that stored field.

00:05:49.292 --> 00:05:52.665
Now we can connect these methods to a real task.

00:05:52.665 --> 00:05:55.476
Main contains the main method where this

00:05:55.476 --> 00:05:57.303
standalone example starts.

00:05:57.303 --> 00:06:00.465
Let's create the Guide reading job with three

00:06:00.465 --> 00:06:03.839
pages, then keep a second reference to that same

00:06:03.839 --> 00:06:06.017
object using the NamedJob type.

00:06:20.154 --> 00:06:23.154
Reading and job refer to one ReadingJob object.

00:06:23.154 --> 00:06:25.898
Their declared types differ, so they do not

00:06:25.898 --> 00:06:28.707
offer the same set of calls to the compiler.

00:06:28.707 --> 00:06:31.579
We'll use reading for its heading method, and

00:06:31.579 --> 00:06:34.259
job for the shared description and minutes

00:06:34.259 --> 00:06:34.962
operations.

00:06:34.962 --> 00:06:37.004
Let's print those three reports.

00:06:50.943 --> 00:06:53.975
The program now connects shared state, an

00:06:53.975 --> 00:06:56.711
override, and a required calculation.

00:06:56.711 --> 00:07:00.260
Let's click Run and compare its reports with the

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:01.443
task we planned.

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:09.779
Job: Guide comes from heading, which obtains the

00:07:09.779 --> 00:07:12.246
stored name through the protected getter.

00:07:12.246 --> 00:07:15.135
Guide reading comes from the override: it reuses

00:07:15.135 --> 00:07:17.783
the base description and appends the suffix.

00:07:17.783 --> 00:07:20.671
Minutes: six comes from ReadingJob's calculation

00:07:20.671 --> 00:07:21.634
for three pages.

00:07:21.634 --> 00:07:24.041
The calls through job reach the concrete

00:07:24.041 --> 00:07:26.569
object's implementations, even though that

00:07:26.569 --> 00:07:28.615
reference is declared as NamedJob.

00:07:29.367 --> 00:07:32.100
We have traced the complete Guide task.

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:35.114
Let's keep the class behavior unchanged and

00:07:35.114 --> 00:07:38.337
create a task named Checklist with four pages,

00:07:38.337 --> 00:07:41.701
so you can apply the same reasoning to different

00:07:41.701 --> 00:07:42.192
inputs.

00:07:49.444 --> 00:07:52.194
Before we run it, predict all three lines.

00:07:52.194 --> 00:07:54.419
Which method supplies the heading?

00:07:54.419 --> 00:07:57.300
How does the override build the description?

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:59.853
Which page count and rate determine the

00:07:59.853 --> 00:08:00.442
duration?

00:08:00.442 --> 00:08:03.519
Pause here if you need time to trace the calls.

00:08:11.639 --> 00:08:14.433
Let's click Run and check your prediction

00:08:14.433 --> 00:08:15.864
against the new task.

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:23.749
The reports are Job: Checklist, Checklist

00:08:23.749 --> 00:08:25.538
reading, and Minutes: eight.

00:08:25.538 --> 00:08:28.540
The new object stores Checklist and four pages.

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:31.478
The heading and description use that name, and

00:08:31.478 --> 00:08:34.417
the required minutes implementation calculates

00:08:34.417 --> 00:08:35.375
four times two.

00:08:35.375 --> 00:08:38.441
Changing the input did not require a new version

00:08:38.441 --> 00:08:40.230
of the shared base behavior.

00:08:40.948 --> 00:08:43.308
We used inheritance to share the name and

00:08:43.308 --> 00:08:45.726
working methods, an override to extend the

00:08:45.726 --> 00:08:48.490
description, and an abstract method to require a

00:08:48.490 --> 00:08:49.526
concrete estimate.

00:08:49.526 --> 00:08:52.059
Both constructor bodies and the later method

00:08:52.059 --> 00:08:53.728
calls worked with one object.

00:08:53.728 --> 00:08:56.204
For a different kind of job, consider which

00:08:56.204 --> 00:08:58.737
information could stay in NamedJob and which

00:08:58.737 --> 00:09:01.270
duration rule the new subclass would need to

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:01.673
supply.
